# ─── 1. JUNK — files a malformed shell redirect leaves behind ─────────────
# ONE RULE PER CLASS, NOT ONE RULE PER INSTANCE.
#
# The leak's SOURCE was fixed long ago: a redirect target is written as
# `cmd > file 2>&1`, never as a bare `> word`. What is left to catch is the
# SHAPE of what the leak left behind, and the shape is regular:
#
#   Class A — a ONE-character name.   `0`, `1`, `6`.
#   Class B — a bare identifier of two or more characters with NO dot in it.
#     `global`, `commit`, `STALE_THRESHOLD_DAYS`, `1800s`, `str`.
#   Class C — a name containing a character no file may legitimately contain:
#     a quote, a bracket, a brace, a parenthesis, a percent, a dollar, a
#     percent.   `dict[str`, `agent('Execute')`, `80%`, `1\``.
#
#   Class D — a dotted version number.   `0.120.0`.
#
# Every legitimate root file in this repository has an extension (`README.md`,
# `pytest.ini`, `uv.lock`) or is a dotfile (`.gitignore`, `.python-version`),
# so a root name outside these four classes is junk by construction, not by
# name. That is what makes one rule per class possible: the previous file
# carried ~150 one-line patterns for individual junk names (`/None`, `/str`,
# `/Path`, `/{*}`, `/frontmatter.dumps()`, `/emit`, `/no`, `/but`, `/80%`,
# `/word`, …) and every one of them is covered by the four rules below.
#
# A WORD ON THE SYNTAX, because it is the whole difference between working
# and not working:
#
#   `[A-Za-z0-9_]*`  is the class FOLLOWED BY A WILDCARD — it matches
#                    "README.md" too, because the wildcard swallows the dot.
#                    "No dot" is written `[^.]`.
#   `[*]`            is a class containing a literal asterisk, NOT a
#                    wildcard. A wildcard is a bare `*`.
#
# Class A matches a root DIRECTORY as readily as a file (`src`, `docs`,
# `data`…), and git never descends into a directory it excluded — so the
# October and November specification series stayed untracked for two months
# for exactly that reason. A negation whose pattern ends in "/" matches
# DIRECTORIES ONLY, so these re-include them: the last rule to match is the
# one that decides, and a junk file is not a directory, so the junk stays
# ignored. Every rule further down decides the directories it means.
/[A-Za-z0-9_]
/[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_][^.]*
/*[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]*
/[0-9]*.[0-9]*
/[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_]
!/[A-Za-z0-9_]*/
!/*/
# The SAME classes, applied to a root DIRECTORY, and placed HERE — after the
# two re-inclusions above, because they are what let the junk back in.
#
# `!/*/` re-includes every root directory, so a directory whose NAME carries a
# character no file may legitimately contain (`$FT`, a shell-redirect leftover)
# came back: git descended into it, and the class rules above only match a
# ROOT NAME — `/*[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]*` cannot match `$FT/.claude/loop/progress.md`
# because `*` never crosses a `/`. The proof was tracked: one junk file
# survived the shape sweep because it sat one directory deep.
#
# The trailing slash makes it a DIRECTORY rule: git never descends into a
# directory it excluded, so the whole tree stays out. A legitimate root
# directory holds only letters, digits, `_`, `.` and `-` — every one of them
# is inside the class, so no legitimate directory matches this.
# The drift test `test_no_tracked_file_is_ignored` holds this honest.
/*[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]*/
# `strategics/` holds the PT-BR strategic prose, and it IS published: the
# vault excerpt names these files as the full grounding
# (`vault/_strategics_excerpt.md`), and the loader that reads them ships.
#
# It is the one root directory the shape rules above cannot leave alone.
# Class B — a bare identifier with no dot — matches the DIRECTORY
# `strategics`, and git never descends into a directory it excluded: so every
# file beneath it was invisible to `git add`, and the index sweep took the
# prose out along with the retired runtime. The directory and the prose are
# named back explicitly. The vendored third-party fork inside it stays
# excluded (§6).
!/strategics/
!/strategics/*.md
# The handful of extension-less names a repository may legitimately carry.
# The legitimate root files. Every one of them carries an extension, so the
# classes above cannot separate them from the junk on shape alone — and a
# rule that hides a REAL file is worse than a rule that shows a junk one.
# The drift test `test_no_tracked_file_is_ignored` holds this list honest.
!/README.md
!/AGENTS.md
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/CLAUDE.md
!/constraints.txt
!/mypy.ini
!/omo.json
!/pyproject.toml
!/pytest.ini
!/uv.lock
!/LICENSE
!/LICENCE
!/NOTICE
!/Makefile
!/Dockerfile
# A dotted module path (`ast.FunctionDef`) has a dot, and so does every
# legitimate root file — the classes cannot separate them. Kept explicit,
# and kept short: a new one of this shape is a signal to widen the rule.
/ast.FunctionDef

# ─── 1b. THE SAME CLASSES, AT DEPTH ───────────────────────────────────────
# The leaks landed at depth too. A root-anchored pattern never reaches a
# subdirectory, so the classes are repeated where they actually accumulate.
# `src/ikigai/` is the only tree that collected them.
/src/-
/src/[0-9]*
src/ikigai/[A-Za-z0-9_]
src/ikigai/[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_][^.]*
src/ikigai/[0-9]*.[0-9]*
src/ikigai/*[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]*
!src/ikigai/[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/
# The Poetry package IS published: the manifest and the lock are what the
# container installs the service's dependencies from, and the four documents
# are the package's own knowledge base. The class rules above swallow every
# top-level name in this tree, so they are named back one by one.
!/src/ikigai/pyproject.toml
!/src/ikigai/poetry.lock
!/src/ikigai/AGENTS.md
!/src/ikigai/README.md
!/src/ikigai/CHANGELOG.md
!/src/ikigai/SPEC.md
# The PUBLISHED PYTHON of this package — the strategics loader the image
# installs. The class rules above swallow the tree's name: "src" is a bare
# identifier, so Class B matches it, and the re-inclusion on line 112 is a
# DIRECTORY rule that brings the directory back to git but leaves a file
# matched deeper down excluded from ruff's walk.
#
# That divergence is not cosmetic. It made `ruff check src/` — the CI gate of
# this very package — resolve to ZERO files: a gate that checks nothing is
# green forever, and a real `I001` was hiding in the loader the whole time.
#
# So the published files are named back one by one, like the documents above.
# The retired trees under this root are excluded again further down (§7), which
# is after this rule — the last rule to match is the one that decides.
!/src/ikigai/src/
!/src/ikigai/src/strategics/
!/src/ikigai/src/strategics/__init__.py
!/src/ikigai/src/strategics/loader.py
!/src/ikigai/src/strategics/frontmatter_compat.py
# Scratch artifacts from ad-hoc CLI and debugging sessions.
/src/ikigai/*.log
/src/ikigai/chat_*.txt
/src/ikigai/crud_*.txt
/src/ikigai/crud_*.py
/src/ikigai/gw_status*.txt
/src/ikigai/ikigai.ps1
/src/ikigai/ikigai_help.txt
/src/ikigai/list_out.txt
/src/ikigai/mypy_output.txt
/src/ikigai/scripts/parse_mypy.py
/src/ikigai/scripts/strip_unused_ignores.py
/src/ikigai/scripts/test_regex.py
# The relative vault root a test run creates (cwd=src/ikigai), and the
# runtime DBs that belong in `data/` at the repository root.
/src/ikigai/vault/
/src/ikigai/data/
/pytestdebug.log
/vault/*.log
# A transcript dump: the session runner wrote its own log beside the code.
# Its name is only made of legal characters, so the classes above cannot see
# it — the shape is the only tell.
*this-session-is-being-continued*
# Literal-tilde dirs (a `~` typed in a path that was replayed as a literal
# string). These are NOT user-home expansions; treat `~` as a component.
**/~/**
**/~*.db

# ─── 2. RUNTIME STATE — regenerated by the daemons and the test suite ────
# The harness's own directory: pi reads the PROJECT configuration from `.pi/`
# (the MCP servers in `.pi/mcp.json`, the skills in `.pi/skills/`, the prompt
# templates in `.pi/prompts/`). The configuration is PUBLISHED — it is the
# wiring between the harness and the api — so the directory is excluded by its
# CONTENTS and the configuration named back.
#
# The ORDER here is the whole thing, and it is the opposite of what it looks
# like. A rule that excludes a DIRECTORY stops git from descending into it,
# and a file re-included beneath an excluded directory stays invisible — the
# negation cannot take effect, because git never looks. So the directory is
# named back AFTER the rule that excludes its contents: `/.pi/*` matches the
# directory itself (its `*` matches the empty remainder), and `!/.pi/` is the
# last rule that matches it, so git descends. Then the file inside it is
# named back by its own path.
#
# `git add .pi/mcp.json` is what proves it: the drift test asks git which
# rule decided.
/.pi/*
!/.pi/
!/.pi/mcp.json
.atl/
.life/
.omo/
.omh/
.atlas/
.hermes/
.hermes_probe/
.openwiki/
.claude-flow/
.langgraph_api/
.opencode/
/logs/
/node_modules/
/.gitnexus/cache/
/worktrees/
/.worktrees/
/reports/baselines/.passphrase
